    "summary": "Warfield '82 delivers high‑energy jams with Scarlet>Fire & Terrapin, but audience tape limits clarity.",
    "review": "The February 16, 1982 Warfield show is a classic early‑'80s snapshot: the band rides a high‑energy current from the opening \"Shakedown Street\" through a scorching \"Scarlet Begonias > Fire on the Mountain\" that many fans cite as the night’s peak. Brent Mydland slips in a sweet keyboard solo on \"Friend of the Devil,\" and the second set rolls into a monster \"Playing In The Band\" sandwich, featuring a soaring \"Terrapin Station,\" an inspired \"Althea,\" and a wild \"Space\" that teases \"Foxy Lady\" before launching into the final “Good Lovin'.”  The encore “Don’t Ease Me In” rounds it out with that familiar Warfield intimacy.\n\nAll available sources are audience recordings of fair quality, so the mix is muddy—especially Phil’s bass—and there’s a noticeable Dolby‑B choke on one tape. Still, the ambience captures the room’s excitement, and the setlist flow feels tight despite the technical limitations. For listeners who can look past the hiss and occasional bleed, this show offers a solid taste of the Dead’s early‑'80s groove and several memorable jams.",
